1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,759
A fire starts inside a spacecraft.

2
00:00:02,839 --> 00:00:05,546
The crew needs more than a guess.

3
00:00:05,626 --> 00:00:09,527
Before applying a test result, one question
matters: does the

4
00:00:09,607 --> 00:00:11,120
evidence cover these conditions?

5
00:00:12,450 --> 00:00:15,287
We are Team Polaris, from Dhaka, Bangladesh.

6
00:00:15,367 --> 00:00:19,453
Inspired by the North Star, we want to help
explorers

7
00:00:19,533 --> 00:00:21,120
find direction through evidence.

8
00:00:22,450 --> 00:00:26,220
Our team is Shamimul Shimul, team lead;

9
00:00:26,300 --> 00:00:28,420
Soleman Hossain, product lead;

10
00:00:28,500 --> 00:00:31,170
Iman Ahamad, computer vision developer;

11
00:00:31,250 --> 00:00:33,370
Sara Rahman, scientific researcher;

12
00:00:33,450 --> 00:00:36,670
Sihab Mahmud, tech development and design;

13
00:00:36,750 --> 00:00:39,970
and Faisal Mahmud Abir, AI engineer.

14
00:00:40,050 --> 00:00:44,920
Together, we bring research, product
thinking, design, and engineering

15
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:47,120
to the same question.

16
00:00:48,450 --> 00:00:52,420
NASA has already studied how materials burn
in microgravity.

17
00:00:52,500 --> 00:00:56,920
But the evidence lives across technical
reports and experimental tables.

18
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:00,070
Finding a result is only the beginning.

19
00:01:00,150 --> 00:01:04,120
Understanding where that result applies is
the harder task.

20
00:01:05,450 --> 00:01:09,100
A material name alone cannot describe a
spacecraft cabin.

21
00:01:09,180 --> 00:01:12,831
Gravity, oxygen, pressure, airflow, and
sample dimensions all matter

22
00:01:12,911 --> 00:01:14,488
when checking the evidence.

23
00:01:14,568 --> 00:01:19,462
If a test did not record the conditions you
asked about, that

24
00:01:19,542 --> 00:01:21,120
gap must stay visible.

25
00:01:22,450 --> 00:01:27,438
Our challenge is Flame in Freefall:
AI-Powered Fire Safety Insights

26
00:01:27,518 --> 00:01:29,465
from Microgravity Combustion Data.

27
00:01:29,545 --> 00:01:35,546
Our concept is a way to summarize NASA
findings, rank patterns in

28
00:01:35,626 --> 00:01:41,120
the evidence, and interpret what the tests
can actually tell us.

29
00:01:42,450 --> 00:01:43,845
Introducing Will It Burn?

30
00:01:43,925 --> 00:01:46,058
, the front door to FlameScope.

31
00:01:46,138 --> 00:01:49,745
Start with a plain-English question, such
as: will acrylic burn

32
00:01:49,825 --> 00:01:51,220
on the Space Station?

33
00:01:51,300 --> 00:01:55,276
The concept brings the question, the
recorded conditions, and the supporting

34
00:01:55,356 --> 00:01:57,120
evidence into one clear view.

35
00:01:58,450 --> 00:02:01,301
First, read the question into explicit
conditions.

36
00:02:01,381 --> 00:02:04,233
Then check them against NASA test records.

37
00:02:04,313 --> 00:02:08,001
A test counts only when its own row records

38
00:02:08,081 --> 00:02:09,676
every condition requested, together.

39
00:02:09,756 --> 00:02:13,445
We do not combine separate tests to create a

40
00:02:13,525 --> 00:02:15,120
match that never happened.

41
00:02:16,450 --> 00:02:20,557
For the broad acrylic question, the BASS-II
table records twenty

42
00:02:20,638 --> 00:02:22,233
acrylic sheets that burned.

43
00:02:22,313 --> 00:02:25,582
The answer is Burned, tied to those tests.

44
00:02:25,663 --> 00:02:30,608
Each result should lead back to its source,
so the user can

45
00:02:30,688 --> 00:02:33,120
inspect the evidence behind the answer.

46
00:02:34,450 --> 00:02:38,120
Now change the question to a Moon base.

47
00:02:38,200 --> 00:02:41,870
These BASS-II tests did not record lunar
gravity.

48
00:02:41,950 --> 00:02:45,620
The answer becomes No data for that
scenario.

49
00:02:45,700 --> 00:02:50,776
The interface explains the missing condition
and shows nearby evidence, while

50
00:02:50,856 --> 00:02:53,120
keeping the gap in view.

51
00:02:54,450 --> 00:02:57,087
The source is part of the answer.

52
00:02:57,167 --> 00:03:01,745
A report, a table, and a test row travel
with each result.

53
00:03:01,825 --> 00:03:03,298
Missing values remain missing.

54
00:03:03,378 --> 00:03:07,567
That lets a researcher follow the reasoning
and decide what needs

55
00:03:07,647 --> 00:03:09,120
to be checked next.

56
00:03:10,450 --> 00:03:15,953
The wider concept brings four modules
together: a source-linked ChatBot, an
analysis

57
00:03:16,033 --> 00:03:20,141
tool, side-by-side comparison, and FlameViz
to visualize recorded measurements.

58
00:03:20,221 --> 00:03:25,259
These planned modules share one purpose:
make NASA evidence easier to

59
00:03:25,339 --> 00:03:27,120
explore, compare, and understand.

60
00:03:28,450 --> 00:03:32,640
AI has a deliberately limited role: select
relevant evidence, or abstain.

61
00:03:32,720 --> 00:03:35,745
The evidence engine computes the numbers and
verdicts.

62
00:03:35,825 --> 00:03:40,403
Our aim is to help researchers ask better
questions, inspect the support,

63
00:03:40,483 --> 00:03:43,120
and see where more testing is needed.

64
00:03:44,450 --> 00:03:45,563
Will It Burn?

65
00:03:45,643 --> 00:03:47,154
, by Team Polaris.

66
00:03:47,234 --> 00:03:48,347
A clear question.

67
00:03:48,427 --> 00:03:49,540
A traceable answer.

68
00:03:49,620 --> 00:03:50,734
An honest gap.

69
00:03:50,814 --> 00:03:53,120
Our concept for Flame in Freefall.

